WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:02.293
- Everybody who's talking about AI,

00:00:02.293 --> 00:00:04.610
it's so bad, and all this.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.030
These articles were written
about machinery around farming.

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:10.180
We just knew that we were all farming.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.150
We went on to do other shit.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:15.850
And by the way, I don't
feel bad for a truck driver

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.820
who isn't reading headlines for 20 years,

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:20.850
that autonomous trucks are coming.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:22.596
We gave 'em 20 fuckin' years.

00:00:22.596 --> 00:00:23.880
(audience laughs)

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.050
When they cry in 2039,

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:28.480
like, "Oh, the fuckin'
autonomous trucks took me out!",

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.203
I'm gonna be like, dick, you had 20 years!

00:00:30.203 --> 00:00:31.620
(audience laughs)

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.110
You couldn't have learned another skill

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:34.310
along the way, asshole?

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:35.467
(audience cheers)

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:37.740
(electronic music)

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:38.990
You got your perspective.

00:00:40.099 --> 00:00:42.790
(audience roaring)

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.943
I just want to be happy,
don't you want to be happy?

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:48.693
What up?
(upbeat music)

00:00:48.693 --> 00:00:49.526
(audience roaring)

00:00:49.526 --> 00:00:51.276
Thank you, thank you.

00:00:53.405 --> 00:00:54.800
(sighs)

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.400
The Knicks didn't get Zion, so I'm dead.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.543
So, energy's gonna be down
at least one solid notch.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.330
Thanks for having me, I've
been really looking forward

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:07.290
to this event for a lot
of different reasons.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.320
Really took note of the merger,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.240
or the acquisition, a year ago.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.853
As some of you may know,

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.363
I started my career in
the liquor business.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:22.260
I was an immigrant, born
in the former Soviet Union.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.260
Kind of cliche story.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.300
My dad was a stock boy in a liquor store,

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.980
and after not spending
any money for a decade,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.050
eventually he bought a small store.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.270
And in 1996, I launched

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.740
one of the first e-commerce
wine businesses in America.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.533
So, this is a space that
I'm really excited about.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.000
Over the last decade, I've
been running a company

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.410
my brother AJ and I
started, called VaynerMedia,

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:50.060
where we've been working
with Fortune 5,000 companies

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.030
around their marketing behaviors.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:54.030
And I think it's a really interesting time

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.900
to be here today for a
lot of different reasons,

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.210
whether you're a service
provider, an agency,

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:02.417
or a retailer, the mix of
the people in this room,

00:02:02.417 --> 00:02:05.880
you know, when I speak, I think one thing

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.860
that's really helped me
have a speaking career

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.810
is I try to reverse engineer the room,

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.150
try to bring the most value.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.970
I have no clue what I'm about
to talk about for 45 minutes.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.200
It's really a stream of consciousness

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.490
of what's actually happening,

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.440
and if I understand
the makeup of the room,

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.250
how do I bring the most value?

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.470
And that's what's really worked for me,

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.040
and that's what I'm gonna do here now,

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.790
because I think what's
super interesting is,

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.460
for a lot of people in this room,

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.880
as I kind of take a look at it,

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.940
we're at this really
interesting inflection point

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.490
where Internet and consumer behavior

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.840
is at complete and utter scale.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.230
And what that means is,

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.670
we're really into a
new era of opportunity.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.250
You know, to me, the white space

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.710
that technology continues
to create, and always will,

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.840
is really a thing that most businesses

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.120
and people struggle with, because,

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.720
the reality is, is that most
people think about things

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.010
based on what has already happened,

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:06.610
instead of being able to
understand what's about to happen.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:09.480
Which is ironic, because
what's about to happen

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.140
already happened in
history, many times over.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.190
It's just, you have to understand
the pattern recognition

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.670
and deploy it against the current state.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.900
And so for me, if you
understood that jargon,

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.830
for me, what is really interesting

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.970
is that no matter who you
are in this room today,

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:27.830
the one thing I know we can talk about

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.520
is attention of the person
you're trying to reach.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.580
My entire career, I've come to realize,

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.170
has been pretty simple.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.350
I've been chasing the attention

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.150
of who I'm trying to reach,

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.850
and that has worked for me
since I was six years old.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:45.010
Probably my favorite
entrepreneurial story,

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.080
from me, about me, to
myself, in my own head, is,

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.730
when I started my lemonade stand business,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.780
I convinced, manipulated, my friends

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:59.120
to work the lemonade stands themselves.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.740
I kind of scoured the streets
of Edison, New Jersey.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:07.330
And if you think about
how sick, or interesting,

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:09.900
what I'm about to say is,
it was interesting to me,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.670
it was fun for me,

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:16.350
to stand on the grassy
knoll or on the corner

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:20.080
and spend hours watching cars drive by

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.890
to try to figure out
which tree, or which pole,

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:27.480
was the best one for me
to put my lemonade sign on

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:28.440
to sell more lemonade.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.130
That was intriguing to me.

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:31.950
That was exciting for me.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:35.590
And, 37 years later, I
stand here in front of you

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:38.190
in the same place, which is,

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.840
where is the attention of
who I'm trying to reach.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:44.900
I've now lived, you know,
two hardcore careers

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.820
that really map this room.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:48.230
The first 15 years of my career

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.750
was hardcore consumer, right?

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:54.010
Brick and mortar store,
Internet business at scale.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:57.060
You know, I built Wine
Library on the back of email,

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:00.420
how many people here have
done or do email marketing

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.050
in their careers, just raise your hands.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:03.350
Just high, please.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:05.620
This is just gonna be fun for me to say,

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:10.620
in 1996, I had a 300,000
person email newsletter

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.293
that had 93% open rates.

00:05:13.293 --> 00:05:14.890
(audience murmurs)

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:17.300
And, I built my dad's
business from a three

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.040
to a $60 million business
in a seven year period,

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:24.840
with no cash infusion, no
VC, not even a credit line,

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:28.660
and it was because I made
every penny work like a dollar.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.470
And in 1996, that was
actually having a website

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.830
when everybody else was
sending catalogs in the mail,

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:35.980
that was having an
email newsletter, which,

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:38.760
for the kids in the room,
you have to understand,

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:40.590
people thought the whole
Internet was a fad,

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:43.380
let alone email marketing, right?

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.340
And then later, the one
that really scaled me,

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.840
which was, the day Google Adwords started,

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.840
I bought ads at five cents a click

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.192
and owned every fuckin'
wine term in the world.

00:05:52.192 --> 00:05:54.350
(audience laughs)

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:56.400
And it's really interesting, what I'm,

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.090
at some point I'm gonna mention this.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:00.440
I would argue that one of the things

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:02.440
that drives me tremendously

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.530
is the great miss of my career,

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.900
during the great success of my career.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.670
I was so right about Google Adwords,

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:11.950
but I had never been through a pattern

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:15.750
of buying very under-priced
attention at scale,

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.420
that I didn't spend enough
money on Google Adwords.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.850
When I, some of you,
and I know some of you

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.980
that I know well, when
you hear three to 60,

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.420
I'm very proud of that, but, genuinely,

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:28.690
I know I left 100 million
in revenue on the table,

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:30.430
because I did too much direct mail,

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:34.270
radio, television,
outdoor, a mix of media,

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:36.470
when I, you know, it's
funny to say this in Vegas,

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:38.090
I don't play poker, but there's something

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:39.180
that's very easy to understand.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.220
When you have the best hand
and you know it, you go all-in.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:45.744
I knew I had the best hand with Adwords,

00:06:45.744 --> 00:06:48.630
I just didn't, let me rephrase,

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:50.380
in hindsight, I had the best hand.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:51.810
I knew it was great.

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:53.480
I just didn't know how great it was.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:55.380
And so as you can imagine,

00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:00.240
when consumer, mobile,
social, Internet came along,

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.900
the pattern recognition
that I learned about email,

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:04.850
which then became about Google Adwords,

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.860
then I started the first, legitimately,

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:10.930
one of the first 10
long-form YouTube shows

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.190
in the first months of YouTube,

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:14.733
which was a 20 minute
show of me tasting wine.

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:18.870
Yeah, best gig I ever had in my career.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:19.950
(audience laughs)

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:22.040
I sat in front of a camera for 25 minutes,

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:23.820
trying four bottles of wine,

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:25.948
and it completely changed
the course of my career.

00:07:25.948 --> 00:07:27.650
(audience laughs)

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:29.300
But, that was the first time I learned

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:33.100
that organic, non-paid media,
could impact my business.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:35.110
And so it's really been one long game

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:37.990
for me to get to this
moment of understanding.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.040
It's the same reason
that I'm pot committed,

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:43.280
completely obsessed with the tollbooth

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:44.620
that the voice devices are gonna be

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:46.270
in our society in a decade.

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.580
The amount of transactions

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:50.110
and decisions that will be made

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:52.440
through Google Home or Alexa,

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:53.890
because it's gonna save us time,

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:55.980
and we will always choose convenience.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:58.360
This is a really fun time
for me in our society,

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:00.070
where everybody's talking about privacy,

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:00.903
and I actually know

00:08:00.903 --> 00:08:02.360
that you don't give a shit about privacy.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:03.530
(audience laughs)

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:04.363
I mean it.

00:08:04.363 --> 00:08:06.690
Humans prove it every single day.

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:09.950
But we care about time,
and we love convenience.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:10.803
And so,

00:08:12.295 --> 00:08:13.420
(audience laughs)

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:14.460
got you, bro.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.590
And so, what I really want to try

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:18.850
to talk about is a couple things.

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:20.310
For my own cadence, to know

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:21.300
where I want to go with this talk,

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.770
how many people here are in B2B?

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:24.603
Raise your hands?

00:08:24.603 --> 00:08:25.436
- [Audience Member] What?

00:08:25.436 --> 00:08:26.580
- (laughs) Nice shout.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:27.503
And B2C?

00:08:28.381 --> 00:08:29.230
All right, okay.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:31.330
So, I'm gonna talk about both.

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:33.240
You know, I'll start with B2B.

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:36.230
If you're in this room, I'm
extremely excited right now,

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:38.380
I am flabbergasted what's transpired

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:40.280
over the last six months on LinkedIn,

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:43.820
LinkedIn's behavior on
organic reach on content.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:45.640
If nobody follows you,

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:49.060
if you've never posted a
piece of content on LinkedIn,

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:50.830
your ability, tonight, probably not,

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:52.070
'cause you're gonna go out, but,

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:53.600
when you get back home,

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.210
to put a piece of content,

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:57.870
whether in long-form
written word, in video,

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.000
or uploading an audio file,

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.600
the amount of organic
reach that post will get,

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.810
if you have even a couple
hundred connections on LinkedIn,

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:09.210
would blow your mind.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:12.780
It's most similar to 2007, 2009 Twitter.

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:14.700
It's most similar, actually,

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.940
to 2011 to 2013 Facebook.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:21.160
And I couldn't be more
passionate for the whole room,

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:22.760
because what's been even
more interesting to me

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.210
as I've gone deep in the last six months,

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:27.430
even non-business content,

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:30.840
like if you sell meal plans,

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:32.940
posting on LinkedIn right now

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:35.760
is remarkable organic reach.

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:39.100
And I couldn't be more
passionate, definitely,

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:41.880
to push everybody who's
in the B2B world here,

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:44.740
because the ROI on acting
like a media company

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:46.390
and not an advertiser on LinkedIn,

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:47.920
I'll get into that in a minute,

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.870
is staggering, and genuinely will go away,

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:53.330
because it's one big
game of cat and mouse,

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:55.500
one big game of cops and robbers.

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:58.640
The organic reach is
through the roof right now.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:00.550
Early adapters like me feel it,

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:02.810
we start working it, it
starts getting talked about,

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:03.643
this room starts doing it.

00:10:03.643 --> 00:10:05.740
It's just supply and demand of attention.

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:06.803
It starts growing,

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:11.020
ads start penetrating in,
ad prices start going up,

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:13.080
and then the marketplace becomes proper,

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.730
like Google Adwords is
today, compared to 2002.

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:20.410
It's really, probably,
the most exciting thing

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:22.550
that's happened to a B2B company.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:25.070
It's very difficult, when you're in B2B,

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:27.880
to not be a sales-driven organization.

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:29.450
It's very difficult.

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:33.170
And so the ability to
not be completely sales,

00:10:33.170 --> 00:10:35.730
and to be in actual marketing

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:37.140
is a remarkable opportunity.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.690
And so, instead of the cliche,

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:42.930
overspending at
conferences for your booth,

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:47.110
for print advertising in the B2B magazine,

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:50.960
for all the cliche dumb
shit that B2B marketers do,

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:53.490
to have a moment where
you can get to scale.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.017
And what I mean by scale is,

00:10:55.017 --> 00:10:59.310
the great misunderstanding of
today's marketing landscape

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:01.640
is that the breakthrough is the ability

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:04.000
to produce creative at scale

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:05.980
that is contextual to the decision maker

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:07.990
that you're trying to reach.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:10.140
The number one shortcoming

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:12.560
of the consumer product marketers in here,

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.970
and the B2B marketers, is
there's not enough volume

00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:18.040
of different creative that is contextual

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:20.160
to who you're trying to reach.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:23.510
People spend so much time in
today's world around the math,

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:25.480
and the arbitrage in
our business world today

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:26.840
is around the art.

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:28.600
The math is so good,

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:30.300
the data is so clean,

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:32.660
that it becomes commoditized over time.

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:35.320
The variable of success for
most people in this room

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:39.450
is the content that is
being put into those pipes.

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:42.380
And so as an opening kind of call to arms,

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:44.480
please get serious about LinkedIn.

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:45.600
Please.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:49.620
It will go away, it's
remarkable, and here's the key.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:51.640
When you fill it with content,

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:54.790
you must act like a media company,

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.760
not as an advertiser or salesperson.

00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:59.320
The only way you will penetrate

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:02.820
in a world of this much
content being put out,

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:04.030
is by putting out something

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:06.630
that brings value to your target audience,

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:08.630
not in the form of a sales pitch,

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:10.450
which is what 98% of the content

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:12.500
that fills these pipes is.

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:14.700
Everybody's putting their pamphlet

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:16.360
and their sales pitch in there,

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:18.150
and what you need to become

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:20.687
is somebody who has the ability to say,

00:12:20.687 --> 00:12:23.467
"Hey CIO, here are 11
things that are happening

00:12:23.467 --> 00:12:25.340
"in your world that will save you money,"

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:27.190
that might not even have anything to do

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:28.850
with your SaaS product,

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:30.700
but bring so much value

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:32.580
to the other person on the other end

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:35.280
that it becomes awareness for
what you're actually doing.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:36.980
There has not been a piece
of content that I put out,

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:38.530
actually, this is a remarkable story

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:40.220
based on the context I've just created.

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:42.380
When I started Wine Library TV in 2006,

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:44.270
February 21st, 2006,

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:46.180
when I sent one of my
stock guys to Best Buy

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:48.850
to buy a camera to put up an episode,

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:51.023
I literally thought I was gonna do QVC.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:53.510
Like, my hope,

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:55.380
I'm a fuckin' wine retailer.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:58.340
I was gonna make videos
and sell people stuff.

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:01.890
And so, the camera goes on,

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:03.890
and within 30 seconds, you
know, it's funny for me,

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:06.093
I actually looked at the
first episode the other day,

00:13:06.093 --> 00:13:08.070
