00:45:47.500 --> 00:45:50.530
What you fill it with
becomes the variable.

00:45:50.530 --> 00:45:52.510
But, yeah man, I, you know...

00:45:54.860 --> 00:45:57.720
Marketing is very, very,
very, very understood,

00:45:57.720 --> 00:45:59.700
we're just not doing it in 2019.

00:45:59.700 --> 00:46:02.150
There's too much math,
and there's too much fear.

00:46:03.280 --> 00:46:05.860
- Two weeks ago, we did,
to build up to this event,

00:46:05.860 --> 00:46:07.980
we did a Instagram live takeover,

00:46:07.980 --> 00:46:11.070
and they let me take
over Magento's Instagram.

00:46:11.070 --> 00:46:12.036
- That was a good decision.
- Thank you, yep,

00:46:12.036 --> 00:46:13.081
very good decision.

00:46:13.081 --> 00:46:15.490
(audience laughs)

00:46:15.490 --> 00:46:18.000
A lot of the audience in this
room is actually on Twitter,

00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:19.437
and I got a little pushback, was like,

00:46:19.437 --> 00:46:21.907
"Why would you push us to Instagram

00:46:21.907 --> 00:46:25.070
"for content, when your
audience is already on Twitter?"

00:46:25.070 --> 00:46:27.010
And my reaction was,

00:46:27.010 --> 00:46:29.310
because that's where
human beings are, right?

00:46:29.310 --> 00:46:30.143
- Well--

00:46:30.143 --> 00:46:32.595
- What's your take on sort
of that channel shift,

00:46:32.595 --> 00:46:33.760
where brands exist?

00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:35.440
- I pull in the opposite
directions with this.

00:46:35.440 --> 00:46:36.730
I think it is our job

00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:39.160
to create no friction for the consumer.

00:46:39.160 --> 00:46:40.547
People are always like,
"Gary, you love digital,

00:46:40.547 --> 00:46:41.400
"why do you write books?"

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:43.760
I'm like, 'cause people read 'em.

00:46:43.760 --> 00:46:46.980
You know, so, to me, I
do think it's important

00:46:46.980 --> 00:46:49.670
to not become one-dimensional
in one channel

00:46:49.670 --> 00:46:51.730
that you're not in control of.

00:46:51.730 --> 00:46:53.410
When Instagram is your, I mean,

00:46:53.410 --> 00:46:55.920
the amount of people that
are in deep fuckin' shit

00:46:55.920 --> 00:46:58.330
because they're
completely, only Instagram,

00:46:58.330 --> 00:47:00.890
and when Instagram's organic reach falls

00:47:00.890 --> 00:47:03.970
through the ground or
collapses, they're in deep shit.

00:47:03.970 --> 00:47:05.970
And what's crazy is, we just lived it

00:47:05.970 --> 00:47:07.550
on Facebook, five minutes ago.

00:47:07.550 --> 00:47:10.060
It's not like it should be
any big surprise it's coming.

00:47:10.060 --> 00:47:12.320
But people cry when it happens.

00:47:12.320 --> 00:47:13.500
So, here's what I would say.

00:47:13.500 --> 00:47:15.650
A, I do think it's important

00:47:15.650 --> 00:47:17.140
when you have attention somewhere

00:47:17.140 --> 00:47:19.440
to amortize it into a lot of places,

00:47:19.440 --> 00:47:21.037
so that your core audience is,

00:47:21.037 --> 00:47:23.550
and you, as a business,
is never one-dimensional.

00:47:23.550 --> 00:47:26.640
But on the flip side, I
do think creating friction

00:47:26.640 --> 00:47:29.450
for the consumer to consume
your content is a bad idea.

00:47:29.450 --> 00:47:32.150
The reason I do written
word, audio, and video,

00:47:32.150 --> 00:47:34.690
is I have no idea how
you want to consume me,

00:47:34.690 --> 00:47:37.000
I just want to give you
a chance to consume me.

00:47:37.000 --> 00:47:38.600
So if you've switched to audio

00:47:38.600 --> 00:47:40.640
instead of video watching, 'cause now,

00:47:40.640 --> 00:47:42.380
when you run, that's what you do,

00:47:42.380 --> 00:47:44.090
I'm gonna be there in podcast form.

00:47:44.090 --> 00:47:45.630
If you're sitting at your desk

00:47:45.630 --> 00:47:47.010
for the first hour of each day

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:50.010
and reading LinkedIn,
written word, I'm there.

00:47:50.010 --> 00:47:53.333
So, I'm trying to create zero
friction for consumption.

00:47:54.800 --> 00:47:56.430
- So, one of the questions that came in

00:47:56.430 --> 00:47:59.517
over the app earlier today was,

00:47:59.517 --> 00:48:02.077
"I'm a business, I'm pretty successful,

00:48:02.077 --> 00:48:03.857
"how do I actually engage my customers

00:48:03.857 --> 00:48:05.627
"on social if I do B2B?

00:48:05.627 --> 00:48:08.777
"Are my customers there,
and where do I even begin?"

00:48:09.910 --> 00:48:11.300
- Obviously, I started my talk with that,

00:48:11.300 --> 00:48:12.390
'cause I know a lot of you are in B2B.

00:48:12.390 --> 00:48:16.740
I can't, guys, my last
book, "Crushing It,"

00:48:16.740 --> 00:48:18.330
just came out 18 months ago.

00:48:18.330 --> 00:48:19.360
You're not gonna be able to see it,

00:48:19.360 --> 00:48:20.330
but maybe you can make it out.

00:48:20.330 --> 00:48:22.460
Oh, actually, well now it's,

00:48:22.460 --> 00:48:25.540
in "Crushing It," I don't
even talk about LinkedIn.

00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:27.730
I'm talking about all these platforms,

00:48:27.730 --> 00:48:29.890
I talk more about Musical.ly,

00:48:29.890 --> 00:48:32.210
in a book I wrote 18
months ago, than LinkedIn.

00:48:32.210 --> 00:48:34.113
Didn't even decide to cover it.

00:48:34.970 --> 00:48:37.150
Was thinking about it, and I was like, eh.

00:48:37.150 --> 00:48:39.030
And now I'm sitting here 18 months later,

00:48:39.030 --> 00:48:41.700
and it was the opening
10 minutes of my talk.

00:48:41.700 --> 00:48:44.610
This is what's so fun about
day trading attention.

00:48:44.610 --> 00:48:46.180
You're emotional.

00:48:46.180 --> 00:48:47.740
I can't wait to be onstage being like,

00:48:47.740 --> 00:48:48.990
you do social media?

00:48:48.990 --> 00:48:50.760
Fuck social media!

00:48:50.760 --> 00:48:51.760
And I mean it!
(audience laughs)

00:48:51.760 --> 00:48:53.760
I really could care
less about social media.

00:48:53.760 --> 00:48:55.180
I care about where your attention is,

00:48:55.180 --> 00:48:56.180
where it's under-priced,

00:48:56.180 --> 00:48:58.913
and how do I deliver you
value that brings me value?

00:48:59.880 --> 00:49:00.960
Very basic.

00:49:00.960 --> 00:49:04.010
So, you know, LinkedIn's crazy, bro.

00:49:04.010 --> 00:49:04.860
Honestly.

00:49:04.860 --> 00:49:08.310
It's at such scale, and such virality,

00:49:08.310 --> 00:49:10.680
and such vibrance, because there's not...

00:49:12.080 --> 00:49:15.240
LinkedIn has more attention
than it has content filling it,

00:49:15.240 --> 00:49:17.010
which is why, this is how it works.

00:49:17.010 --> 00:49:18.950
You have to get in
there, guys, you have to.

00:49:18.950 --> 00:49:20.720
So, that and podcasts.

00:49:20.720 --> 00:49:23.480
I think every B2B player in here

00:49:23.480 --> 00:49:26.666
needs to think about doing a podcast,

00:49:26.666 --> 00:49:29.710
minimumly once a month,
preferably once a week,

00:49:29.710 --> 00:49:33.570
and make a podcast that
actually brings value

00:49:33.570 --> 00:49:36.720
to the employees of the
companies you're trying to reach.

00:49:36.720 --> 00:49:39.410
It doesn't even have to,
it could be about golf,

00:49:39.410 --> 00:49:41.960
if you think that you industry over index

00:49:41.960 --> 00:49:45.130
is on loving fucking golf, as executives.

00:49:45.130 --> 00:49:49.070
It just needs the hit,
the CIO, the CTO, the CFO,

00:49:49.070 --> 00:49:51.533
bring value, own that.

00:49:53.420 --> 00:49:55.160
I'm very, very bullish on that.

00:49:55.160 --> 00:49:56.560
And so I do think, I do believe

00:49:56.560 --> 00:49:59.590
that you will see the biggest
companies in the world

00:49:59.590 --> 00:50:03.400
have a editor in chief,
along with a CMO, and a,

00:50:03.400 --> 00:50:05.790
you know, chief brand
officer, over the next decade.

00:50:05.790 --> 00:50:07.100
We will absolutely become

00:50:07.100 --> 00:50:10.460
much more publisher than advertiser,

00:50:10.460 --> 00:50:15.180
because of consumers'
non-interest in advertising.

00:50:15.180 --> 00:50:16.520
And when you do advertising, for example,

00:50:16.520 --> 00:50:17.450
something I'm very proud of,

00:50:17.450 --> 00:50:19.090
and I think some of you saw it,

00:50:19.090 --> 00:50:21.270
'cause, jeez, it really got consumed.

00:50:21.270 --> 00:50:23.480
Couple weeks ago, at VaynerMedia,

00:50:23.480 --> 00:50:26.180
we were the agency, creative
lead, and the production house

00:50:26.180 --> 00:50:28.720
behind that Dwyane Wade
Budweiser spot, right?

00:50:28.720 --> 00:50:31.460
Where we retired his jersey
and he did the jersey swaps.

00:50:31.460 --> 00:50:32.960
If you're gonna do advertising today,

00:50:32.960 --> 00:50:34.240
it needs to be a three minute video

00:50:34.240 --> 00:50:35.680
that people want to watch,

00:50:35.680 --> 00:50:38.380
versus a six second pre-roll
that nobody wants to see.

00:50:39.370 --> 00:50:41.250
And so, that's what I think about it.

00:50:41.250 --> 00:50:43.050
- Another question that came in,

00:50:43.050 --> 00:50:44.870
was, "How do you," and you covered this

00:50:44.870 --> 00:50:46.117
a little bit earlier, "How do you deal

00:50:46.117 --> 00:50:47.837
"with entrenched bureaucracy
in an organization?

00:50:47.837 --> 00:50:49.500
"Is it better to bail?"

00:50:49.500 --> 00:50:50.980
- [Gary] Yes.

00:50:50.980 --> 00:50:52.630
- Okay.
(audience laughs)

00:50:52.630 --> 00:50:53.725
- Quick.

00:50:53.725 --> 00:50:54.750
(audience cheers)

00:50:54.750 --> 00:50:57.180
And I'll say why, and I'll say why.

00:50:57.180 --> 00:50:59.140
And I really believe it.

00:50:59.140 --> 00:51:02.440
I think the reality is,

00:51:02.440 --> 00:51:06.150
almost every business cares
about short-term economics.

00:51:06.150 --> 00:51:08.200
And when you care about
short-term economics,

00:51:08.200 --> 00:51:10.743
it almost always goes
against what's right.

00:51:13.520 --> 00:51:16.010
If you were only raising your
child until they were four

00:51:16.010 --> 00:51:18.610
and they would leave, you
would raise them differently.

00:51:18.610 --> 00:51:19.800
And unfortunately, we have a lot of,

00:51:19.800 --> 00:51:21.420
and I don't blame, I used to blame people,

00:51:21.420 --> 00:51:23.260
but then I'm like, no, this
actually the structure.

00:51:23.260 --> 00:51:25.500
I can't be mad at this CEO.

00:51:25.500 --> 00:51:28.300
She's worked for 37 years,
hard, to get into this spot,

00:51:28.300 --> 00:51:31.070
and she cares about maximizing
short-term economics,

00:51:31.070 --> 00:51:32.140
because when she leaves,

00:51:32.140 --> 00:51:33.660
she needs to sell out her options,

00:51:33.660 --> 00:51:35.940
and she wants it to be as
highly valuable as possible,

00:51:35.940 --> 00:51:37.320
she doesn't care about that company

00:51:37.320 --> 00:51:38.860
for the next 50 years anymore.

00:51:38.860 --> 00:51:39.880
She's an executive.

00:51:39.880 --> 00:51:41.593
It's not a family business.

00:51:42.580 --> 00:51:44.660
You're not gonna change it.

00:51:44.660 --> 00:51:46.163
Really, you're not.

00:51:47.600 --> 00:51:49.760
If you work in a company that is required

00:51:49.760 --> 00:51:51.050
to create short-term economics,

00:51:51.050 --> 00:51:54.020
whether that is because they're
a publicly traded company,

00:51:54.020 --> 00:51:56.070
they're a private company looking to sell,

00:51:56.070 --> 00:51:57.980
so they're trying to maximize EBITDA,

00:51:57.980 --> 00:52:01.163
or they're an early stage
company trying to raise capital,

00:52:02.220 --> 00:52:03.220
everything's fucked.

00:52:05.270 --> 00:52:07.400
So yes, if you do not believe,

00:52:07.400 --> 00:52:11.560
the beauty of being an
employee is, you get to leave.

00:52:11.560 --> 00:52:13.020
The lonely part of being an entrepreneur

00:52:13.020 --> 00:52:14.830
and owning your company
is, it's your fault,

00:52:14.830 --> 00:52:16.480
and there's nobody else to blame.

00:52:17.590 --> 00:52:19.920
Right?
(audience cheering)

00:52:19.920 --> 00:52:21.690
The great part of being an employee is,

00:52:21.690 --> 00:52:23.220
even if you suck, when you get fired,

00:52:23.220 --> 00:52:24.799
you're like, "My boss was a dick."

00:52:24.799 --> 00:52:27.630
(audience laughs)

00:52:27.630 --> 00:52:29.530
You don't have to take an L, you know?

00:52:30.620 --> 00:52:32.790
- One question came in,
it sort of appealed to me,

00:52:32.790 --> 00:52:34.368
I'm kind of a sneakerhead, and--

00:52:34.368 --> 00:52:35.961
- Yeah, I saw, crazy.

00:52:35.961 --> 00:52:37.047
(audience laughs)

00:52:37.047 --> 00:52:40.360
- And, they were asking why K-Swiss,

00:52:40.360 --> 00:52:43.450
and I think that kind of, you
did a collab with K-Swiss.

00:52:43.450 --> 00:52:44.730
You have a number of shoes.

00:52:44.730 --> 00:52:48.210
And I have to wonder, the
question actually asked,

00:52:48.210 --> 00:52:49.370
you know, "Did you sell out?"

00:52:49.370 --> 00:52:52.560
Or is it, the short-term economics is,

00:52:52.560 --> 00:52:53.580
could you tell that story a little bit?

00:52:53.580 --> 00:52:54.413
- I sure can.

00:52:54.413 --> 00:52:56.800
My number one reason
for building my agency

00:52:56.800 --> 00:52:59.640
is because I thought that
everybody's in big trouble.

00:52:59.640 --> 00:53:03.300
I mean, I wish I was making video in 2011,

00:53:04.650 --> 00:53:05.690
when I really made this decision.

00:53:05.690 --> 00:53:07.650
My basic thought was,
Netflix was gonna win,

00:53:07.650 --> 00:53:11.330
Facebook was gonna win, you
know, Amazon was gonna win.

00:53:11.330 --> 00:53:12.380
So I did really well

00:53:12.380 --> 00:53:14.950
in investing public
companies, private companies,

00:53:14.950 --> 00:53:16.650
and I thought the best way for me

00:53:16.650 --> 00:53:17.580
to take advantage of that was

00:53:17.580 --> 00:53:20.290
to build the best
communication company machine,

00:53:20.290 --> 00:53:22.440
as I call it, infrastructure,

00:53:22.440 --> 00:53:25.840
to then buy nostalgic
brands, and reboot them.

00:53:25.840 --> 00:53:27.110
I believe that nostalgia

00:53:27.110 --> 00:53:30.680
is the number one under-priced
thing in human behavior.

00:53:30.680 --> 00:53:33.910
And so, K-Swiss, for me, was
a really interesting deal.

00:53:33.910 --> 00:53:35.310
Couple things.

00:53:35.310 --> 00:53:37.850
They reached out to me, and so,

00:53:37.850 --> 00:53:40.920
like anything else, you kind of vet that.

00:53:40.920 --> 00:53:41.753
Number two,

00:53:43.800 --> 00:53:46.380
K-Swiss meant nothing, in culture,

00:53:46.380 --> 00:53:48.550
when they reached out
to me, so any uptick,

00:53:48.550 --> 00:53:51.340
I was gonna get
disproportionate credit for.

00:53:51.340 --> 00:53:54.068
If I did a deal with Nike or Reebok,

00:53:54.068 --> 00:53:55.970
it would've been cool, but none of

00:53:55.970 --> 00:53:57.670
what I'm actually trying to achieve.

00:53:57.670 --> 00:53:58.570
I don't want to be cool

00:53:58.570 --> 00:54:00.200
and be and entrepreneur with sneakers,

00:54:00.200 --> 00:54:04.330
I need to find test cases,
for me to test my thesis,

00:54:04.330 --> 00:54:06.790
on, am I actually building
an effective machine

00:54:06.790 --> 00:54:08.823
that's gonna allow me to buy, you know,

00:54:09.740 --> 00:54:11.360
remember Jams shorts?

00:54:11.360 --> 00:54:12.410
I don't know why I still want

00:54:12.410 --> 00:54:13.721
to buy that brand and bring it back.

00:54:13.721 --> 00:54:14.554
(audience laughs)

00:54:14.554 --> 00:54:15.387
For the youngsters, it was like

00:54:15.387 --> 00:54:17.840
a first version of Zubaz,
but in shorts form.

00:54:17.840 --> 00:54:18.703
You know, anyway,

00:54:20.190 --> 00:54:22.380
K-Swiss was one of the best
things that I've ever done.

00:54:22.380 --> 00:54:24.700
One, it was all upside.

00:54:24.700 --> 00:54:27.820
It was either gonna work, and
it would've taught me a lot,

00:54:27.820 --> 00:54:29.530
or it wasn't gonna
work, and all my friends

00:54:29.530 --> 00:54:31.090
would've made fun of me, for being a dick

00:54:31.090 --> 00:54:32.257
and thinking I could
make a sneaker, right?

00:54:32.257 --> 00:54:34.370
- Right.
- So it was like, no risk.

00:54:36.143 --> 00:54:39.023
No, I don't feel like I sold out on it.

00:54:40.489 --> 00:54:43.510
- No, there was a mutual
value there, right?

00:54:43.510 --> 00:54:44.610
- That's right.

00:54:44.610 --> 00:54:45.660
100%.

00:54:45.660 --> 00:54:48.780
And K-Swiss just sold to a Chinese company

00:54:48.780 --> 00:54:50.523
for a ton of money, way
more that they would've

00:54:50.523 --> 00:54:51.580
if they didn't do the deal with me.

00:54:51.580 --> 00:54:54.190
So, I feel good about it, they won,

00:54:54.190 --> 00:54:56.850
I learned a fuckload for
what I'm gonna do next.

00:54:56.850 --> 00:55:01.010
I now look at apparel as an
actual category that I may buy

00:55:01.010 --> 00:55:03.210
when the economy collapses,
instead of just food,

00:55:03.210 --> 00:55:04.580
which is where I was originally.

00:55:04.580 --> 00:55:06.150
So I've given myself options,

00:55:06.150 --> 00:55:08.740
when this next economic decline happens,

00:55:08.740 --> 00:55:11.163
'cause that's when I'm
gonna strike like a cobra.

00:55:12.010 --> 00:55:15.506
I, literally, I can't wait
for the next bears' turn.

00:55:15.506 --> 00:55:17.310
(audience laughs)

00:55:17.310 --> 00:55:18.810
I mean it, I mean it.

00:55:18.810 --> 00:55:23.640
I think that there's a
lot of bullshit going on.

00:55:23.640 --> 00:55:27.520
I think there's a lot of D
players, getting A results,

00:55:27.520 --> 00:55:30.363
but who are being fakely propped up.

00:55:31.440 --> 00:55:33.680
- So I think we have time
for one more question.

00:55:33.680 --> 00:55:35.100
This one was sort of heavy,

00:55:35.100 --> 00:55:36.110
I'd love your take on it.

00:55:36.110 --> 00:55:36.943
- [Gary] Sure.

00:55:36.943 --> 00:55:38.440
- Do you think we're in an AI arms race,

00:55:38.440 --> 00:55:40.440
and where do you think that ends?

00:55:40.440 --> 00:55:42.570
- You know, one thing I
really am passionate about

00:55:42.570 --> 00:55:44.330
that's evolved for me,
and I'm thankful for it,

00:55:44.330 --> 00:55:47.643
is I try not to talk about
shit that I don't know.

00:55:48.610 --> 00:55:51.020
So, the answer is I'm not sure.

00:55:51.020 --> 00:55:55.530
I haven't done enough homework on AI

00:55:55.530 --> 00:55:58.680
to answer that thoughtfully,
like the other questions.

00:55:58.680 --> 00:56:02.450
I think it's inevitable,
the technology's inevitable.

00:56:02.450 --> 00:56:06.050
But I think people grossly
underestimate human beings.

00:56:06.050 --> 00:56:07.800
We evolve into other shit.

00:56:07.800 --> 00:56:09.950
I think AI is gonna do a lot of dumb shit

00:56:09.950 --> 00:56:11.650
that we shouldn't be doing anyway.

00:56:12.700 --> 00:56:16.040
And it's going to be impactful,

00:56:16.040 --> 00:56:19.320
but not the way, I don't think this,

00:56:19.320 --> 00:56:21.200
I think this could be a precursor

00:56:21.200 --> 00:56:22.990
to the robots killing us.

00:56:22.990 --> 00:56:24.280
(audience laughs)

00:56:24.280 --> 00:56:25.720
Which I do think is inevitable.

